Secured and Monitored Web Infrastructure
==========================================

Overview
--------
This builds on the three-server setup (load balancer + 2 servers, each
with a web server, app server, code, and database) by adding
security and monitoring: 3 firewalls, an SSL certificate for HTTPS,
and 3 monitoring clients.

What got added, and why
-------------------------
- 3 firewalls (one in front of the load balancer, one on each of the
  2 servers): each piece gets its own protection, so even if traffic
  reaches the network, unwanted ports/connections get blocked before
  hitting the actual services.
- 1 SSL certificate (on the load balancer, where HTTPS ends):
  lets the site run over HTTPS, encrypting traffic between the user
  and the infrastructure.
- 3 monitoring clients (one on the load balancer, one on each server):
  each piece reports its own stats (CPU, memory, disk, traffic) to the
  monitoring service, so you can see the health of everything.

What are firewalls for?
A firewall controls what traffic can come in and go out, based on
rules you set - blocking bad/unwanted traffic while letting the good
stuff through. It protects the servers from unwanted connections.

Why serve traffic over HTTPS?
HTTPS encrypts the data between the user's browser and the server, so
it can't be easily read or messed with by someone snooping on the
connection (like on public Wi-Fi). It also confirms the server is who
it says it is, via the SSL certificate.

What's monitoring for?
Monitoring tracks the health, performance, and uptime of the
infrastructure in real time - so you can catch problems (high CPU,
downtime, slow responses, errors) early, before they really hurt
users.

How does the monitoring tool collect data?
A small agent (monitoring client) runs on each server. It gathers
local stats (CPU, memory, disk, network, app-specific metrics) and
sends that data to the monitoring service on a regular basis, where
you can view it and set up alerts.

Monitoring the web server's QPS (Queries Per Second)
To track QPS, you'd set up a custom metric that counts how many
requests Nginx handles per second (e.g. by reading the Nginx logs or
using a plugin that exposes that stat), then send that number to the
monitoring service so you can graph it and get alerted if it spikes
or drops.

Issues with this setup
--------------------------------
Why is ending SSL at the load balancer a problem?
Traffic between the load balancer and the backend servers is no
longer encrypted (it gets decrypted at the load balancer). So if
someone gets into the internal network, they could read that traffic
in plain text.

Why is having only one MySQL server that can accept writes a problem?
That one write-capable (Primary) database is a weak point - if it
goes down, nothing can be written anywhere until it's fixed or a
Replica gets promoted to take over, which disrupts any write
operation in the meantime.

Why might having all the same components on every server be a problem?
If every server runs the web server, app server, and database
together, a spike from one of them (say, the database working hard)
can slow down or crash the others on that same machine, since they're
all fighting over the same CPU, memory, and disk. It also makes it
harder to scale or secure each piece on its own.
